{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00420 - MONDO_2313",
  "verbalization": "Promazine, a centrally acting antipsychotic, is linked to Vernal Conjunctivitis through a series of indications that ultimately converge on this condition. The evidence suggests that Promazine may exert a therapeutic effect on Vernal Conjunctivitis via its interaction with Atopic Conjunctivitis, which in turn is associated with Tromethamine, Emedastine, and Alcaftadine. These compounds are all indicated for Vernal Conjunctivitis, indicating a potential pathway where Promazine could influence the disease through a shared biological mechanism. The presence of multiple pathways with similar clinical associations supports the hypothesis that Promazine may have a role in modulating the inflammatory or allergic processes underlying Vernal Conjunctivitis. The integration of these pathways highlights the possibility that Promazine could act through a mechanism that is not yet fully understood, but which is likely to involve the modulation of inflammatory mediators or immune responses. The evidence is consistent with a mechanistic link, though the exact nature of this mechanism remains to be elucidated.",
